NEW_LEARNING_CONTENT = {
    "dog-parent": {
        "Bringing Your Dog Home": "Bringing a dog home is an exciting experience, but it also comes with new responsibilities. Your dog may feel nervous or confused when entering an unfamiliar environment. Prepare a comfortable space with a bed, food and water bowls, toys, and a safe resting area. Remove harmful objects, electrical wires, cleaning products, and other dangerous items from your dog's reach.\n\nDuring the first few days, allow your dog to explore the surroundings at its own pace. Introduce family members gradually and avoid overwhelming your new companion with too much attention. Establish a regular routine for feeding, sleeping, toilet breaks, and playtime. Patience and consistency will help your dog feel secure and develop a positive connection with its new family.",
        "House Training Basics": "House training teaches your dog where and when to eliminate. Young puppies may need frequent toilet breaks because their bladder control is still developing. Take your dog outside after waking up, eating, drinking, playing, and before bedtime. Choose a consistent toilet area and give your dog enough time to become familiar with it.\n\nPositive reinforcement is one of the most effective ways to develop good toilet habits. Praise your dog and offer a small treat immediately after it uses the correct area. Accidents may happen during training, so clean the area thoroughly and continue following the routine. Never punish or frighten your dog for accidents, as this can create anxiety and make training more difficult.",
        "Feeding & Nutrition": "Proper nutrition supports your dog's growth, energy, immunity, and overall health. Dogs require a balanced diet containing appropriate amounts of protein, fats, carbohydrates, vitamins, minerals, and water. Their nutritional requirements change according to age, breed, body size, activity level, and health condition.\n\nChoose complete and balanced dog food suitable for your dog's life stage. Follow feeding recommendations and adjust portions according to body condition and veterinary advice. Always provide fresh drinking water. Avoid feeding harmful foods such as chocolate, grapes, raisins, onions, garlic, and products containing xylitol. Introduce dietary changes gradually to avoid digestive problems.",
        "Vet Care & Vaccinations": "Regular veterinary care is essential for protecting your dog against diseases and identifying health problems early. Schedule a veterinary examination after bringing your dog home, even if your dog appears healthy. The veterinarian can assess physical development, check for parasites, review medical history, and recommend vaccinations and preventive treatments.\n\nVaccinations protect dogs against several serious infectious diseases, including rabies, distemper, parvovirus, and adenovirus. Puppies generally require a series of vaccinations, followed by boosters according to veterinary recommendations. Keep vaccination records updated and attend scheduled health examinations. Changes in appetite, unusual tiredness, vomiting, diarrhoea, or behavioural changes should be discussed with a veterinarian.",
        "Leash Training": "Leash training teaches your dog to walk safely and comfortably while connected to a leash. Begin by introducing a comfortable, properly fitted collar or harness inside the home. Allow your dog to become familiar with the equipment before attaching the leash. Use treats and encouragement to make the experience enjoyable.\n\nOnce your dog becomes comfortable, practise walking indoors or in a quiet outdoor area. Reward your dog for walking beside you without pulling. If your dog pulls, stop calmly and encourage it to return to your side before continuing. Gradually introduce different walking environments. Regular practice helps develop good walking habits and makes outdoor activities safer.",
        "Socialisation": "Socialisation helps dogs become comfortable around different people, animals, sounds, objects, and environments. A well-socialised dog is more likely to respond calmly to unfamiliar experiences. Begin introducing new experiences gradually, making sure each interaction is positive and appropriate for your dog's age and health.\n\nAllow your dog to meet friendly people and suitable dogs in controlled environments. Introduce everyday sounds such as traffic, household appliances, and outdoor activities without overwhelming your pet. Observe body language carefully because hiding, trembling, turning away, and a tucked tail may indicate discomfort. Respect your dog's boundaries and allow it to approach unfamiliar experiences at its own pace.",
        "Grooming & Building Trust": "Regular grooming helps maintain your dog's coat, skin, teeth, ears, and nails. Brushing removes loose hair and helps prevent tangles, while bathing with dog-specific shampoo keeps the coat clean. Dental hygiene is equally important because poor oral health can cause discomfort and other health problems. Grooming requirements vary depending on coat type and individual needs.\n\nBuilding trust requires patience, consistency, and positive interactions. Spend quality time playing, walking, and training with your dog. Reward good behaviour and avoid shouting or physical punishment. Learn to understand your dog's body language and respect its personal space. When your dog feels safe and understood, it becomes easier to develop a strong and lasting relationship.",
    },
    "cat-parent": {
        "Preparing Your Home for a Cat": "Preparing your home before bringing a cat is important for creating a safe and comfortable environment. Cats need a quiet resting area, clean litter box, food and water bowls, scratching surfaces, and suitable toys. Provide hiding places and elevated spaces where your cat can observe its surroundings and feel secure.\n\nRemove poisonous plants, medicines, cleaning chemicals, and small objects that could be swallowed. Secure windows and balconies to prevent accidental falls or escapes. When your cat arrives, allow it to explore one comfortable room before introducing the rest of the home. A peaceful environment helps reduce stress and supports a smoother adjustment.",
        "Understanding Cat Behaviour": "Cats communicate through body movements, facial expressions, vocalisations, and behaviour. Purring can indicate comfort, although it may also occur when a cat is stressed or unwell. Tail movements, ear positions, eye contact, and body posture provide useful information about a cat's emotional state.\n\nA relaxed cat may have soft eyes, neutral ears, and a comfortable body posture. Flattened ears, hissing, growling, hiding, or a tense body can indicate fear or discomfort. Understanding these signals helps cat parents respond appropriately. Avoid forcing interactions when a cat wants space, and allow it to approach people voluntarily.",
        "Litter Training": "Litter training teaches cats to use a designated area for urination and defecation. Choose a suitable litter box that is large enough for your cat to enter, turn around, and dig comfortably. Place it in a quiet, accessible location away from food and water bowls. Some cats prefer uncovered litter boxes, while others may feel comfortable using covered ones.\n\nIntroduce your cat to the litter box and allow it to explore naturally. Keep the litter clean by removing waste regularly and replacing it as needed. If your cat suddenly stops using the litter box, consider possible stress, cleanliness, or medical issues. Consult a veterinarian if inappropriate elimination continues or is accompanied by signs of discomfort.",
        "Feeding & Nutrition": "Cats are obligate carnivores, meaning they require nutrients naturally obtained from animal-based foods. Their diet must provide essential nutrients such as taurine, animal protein, fatty acids, vitamins, and minerals. Feeding requirements differ between kittens, adult cats, pregnant cats, and senior cats.\n\nChoose complete and balanced cat food suitable for your cat's age and health condition. Provide clean drinking water throughout the day and monitor food portions to maintain a healthy weight. Avoid feeding onions, garlic, chocolate, grapes, raisins, and products containing xylitol. Never assume that a homemade or vegetarian diet automatically provides the nutrients a cat needs.",
        "Vet Care & Vaccinations": "Regular veterinary examinations help maintain your cat's health and identify medical conditions before they become serious. During checkups, veterinarians assess body condition, teeth, eyes, ears, skin, and general physical health. They can also recommend parasite prevention, dental care, and suitable nutrition.\n\nVaccinations protect cats against important infectious diseases such as feline panleukopenia, feline herpesvirus, and feline calicivirus. Rabies vaccination may also be recommended or legally required depending on location. Kittens generally need an initial vaccination series followed by boosters. Maintain vaccination records and seek veterinary advice whenever you notice changes in appetite, behaviour, movement, or litter box habits.",
        "Grooming & Hygiene": "Grooming keeps your cat's coat clean and helps prevent tangles and excessive loose hair. Short-haired cats may require less frequent brushing than long-haired cats, which often need regular grooming to prevent mats. Introduce brushing gradually and use a suitable grooming tool for your cat's coat type.\n\nDental care, nail maintenance, and ear checks are also important. Use cat-specific dental products and never use human toothpaste. Trim nails carefully without cutting the sensitive quick. Check the ears and eyes for unusual discharge, redness, or irritation. If you notice skin problems, persistent itching, or painful grooming areas, consult a veterinarian.",
        "Bonding & Playtime": "Building a relationship with your cat takes patience and understanding. Cats often prefer gentle interactions and may need time before accepting physical affection. Sit near your cat, speak calmly, and allow it to approach you when it feels comfortable. Respect its sleeping and resting areas.\n\nPlaytime provides exercise and mental stimulation. Interactive toys, feather wands, and suitable puzzle feeders can encourage natural hunting behaviours. Provide scratching posts and climbing spaces to support physical activity. Avoid using your hands as toys because this can encourage biting and scratching. Consistent positive interactions help develop trust and strengthen your relationship.",
    },
}

NEW_LEARNING_CONTENT.update({
    "hamster-care": {
        "Preparing a Hamster Home": "Hamsters need a safe, spacious, and stimulating environment where they can explore, burrow, sleep, and exercise. Choose an appropriately sized enclosure with good ventilation and enough floor space. Provide deep, safe bedding that allows natural digging behaviour, along with a suitable nesting area and hiding places.\n\nPlace the enclosure in a quiet location away from direct sunlight, loud noises, and extreme temperatures. Include a solid-surface exercise wheel, safe chew toys, and suitable enrichment materials. Avoid wire flooring and unsafe plastic objects that could cause injuries. A comfortable habitat helps your hamster feel secure and supports its natural behaviours.",
        "Understanding Hamster Behaviour": "Hamsters are small animals with individual personalities and natural behavioural patterns. Most hamsters are active during the evening and night, although their activity schedules can vary. They enjoy exploring, burrowing, collecting food, and running on exercise wheels. Understanding these habits helps owners provide an environment that meets their needs.\n\nHamsters may hide, freeze, squeak, or bite when frightened or disturbed. Some species are naturally solitary and should generally be housed individually to prevent fighting. Avoid waking your hamster suddenly or disturbing it while sleeping. Observe its behaviour regularly to recognise signs of comfort, stress, or illness.",
        "Building Trust with Your Hamster": "Building trust with a hamster requires patience and gentle interaction. When bringing a hamster home, allow it several days to adjust without unnecessary handling. Sit near the enclosure and speak softly so your hamster gradually becomes familiar with your presence.\n\nOnce it appears comfortable, offer a small suitable treat from your hand. Allow it to approach and investigate voluntarily. Avoid chasing, grabbing, or forcing physical contact. Regular, gentle interactions help your hamster associate your presence with positive experiences and gradually become more comfortable around you.",
        "Handling & Interaction": "Hamsters are delicate animals and must be handled carefully. Begin by allowing your hamster to sniff your hand before attempting to lift it. Scoop it gently using both hands and keep it close to a low, secure surface to prevent injuries if it jumps.\n\nNever squeeze your hamster, lift it by its legs or tail, or disturb it while sleeping. Children should handle hamsters only under adult supervision. If your hamster shows signs of fear, such as struggling, squeaking, or attempting to bite, return it safely to its enclosure and try again another time.",
        "Feeding & Nutrition": "A healthy hamster diet should provide suitable protein, fibre, fats, vitamins, and minerals. A nutritionally complete commercial hamster food should form the foundation of its diet. Small quantities of suitable vegetables and occasional safe treats can provide variety.\n\nFresh drinking water must always be available. Avoid foods such as chocolate, onion, garlic, and products containing excessive sugar or salt. Introduce new foods gradually and remove fresh food before it spoils. Because hamsters store food in their cheek pouches and nesting areas, regularly check hidden food supplies to prevent mould and contamination.",
        "Exercise & Enrichment": "Hamsters need daily opportunities to exercise and express natural behaviours. A suitable solid-surface wheel allows them to run safely, while deep bedding supports digging and burrowing. Tunnels, cardboard structures, safe chew materials, and hiding spaces provide additional stimulation.\n\nChange or rotate enrichment materials occasionally to maintain interest without completely disrupting the familiar environment. Supervised exploration in a secure hamster play area may provide additional activity. Avoid exercise balls because they can cause stress, overheating, and injuries. A stimulating enclosure helps prevent boredom and supports physical and mental wellbeing.",
        "Health & Hygiene": "Hamsters need regular health monitoring because small changes can quickly become serious. Observe their eating habits, drinking, movement, breathing, fur condition, and droppings. Warning signs include weight loss, wetness around the tail, unusual lumps, breathing difficulties, excessive scratching, and reduced activity.\n\nSpot-clean soiled bedding regularly and replace bedding as necessary while retaining some clean, familiar nesting material. Hamsters generally do not require routine water baths, which can remove natural oils and cause stress. Sand baths may be suitable for certain species. Consult a veterinarian experienced in small mammals whenever you notice signs of illness.",
    },
    "parrot-care": {
        "Preparing a Home for Your Parrot": "Parrots are intelligent, active birds that require spacious and secure living environments. Choose a cage that allows your parrot to stretch its wings, climb, move comfortably, and play. Provide suitable perches of different diameters, food and water containers, and bird-safe toys.\n\nPlace the cage in a well-ventilated area away from kitchen fumes, smoke, strong fragrances, and direct exposure to extreme temperatures. Avoid non-stick cookware fumes, which can be fatal to birds. Parrots also need supervised time outside their cages in a bird-safe room. A suitable home environment supports their physical and emotional wellbeing.",
        "Understanding Parrot Behaviour": "Parrots communicate through vocalisations, body movements, feather positions, and beak behaviour. They may whistle, chirp, call, or imitate sounds to communicate with their surroundings. Some behaviours indicate excitement, while others may suggest fear, discomfort, or overstimulation.\n\nFeather fluffing, eye pinning, tail movements, and changes in posture should be interpreted in context. A parrot that suddenly becomes unusually quiet, aggressive, or inactive may be experiencing stress or illness. Understanding these communication signals helps owners respond appropriately and create a comfortable relationship with their birds.",
        "Building Trust & Communication": "Trust develops when parrots experience consistent, gentle, and predictable interactions. Begin by spending time near the cage without forcing physical contact. Speak softly and allow the bird to observe you. Offer suitable treats through positive reinforcement when the parrot appears comfortable.\n\nAs trust improves, introduce simple interactions such as stepping onto a hand or responding to familiar sounds. Avoid chasing, grabbing, or punishing your bird. Every parrot has an individual personality and comfort level. Respecting its boundaries helps create a strong relationship based on safety and positive communication.",
        "Feeding & Nutrition": "Parrots require a balanced diet that supports their metabolism, feathers, muscles, and immune system. A suitable formulated pellet diet is often recommended as the main food for many companion parrot species, supplemented with appropriate fresh vegetables and limited fruits. Nutritional requirements vary depending on species, age, and health.\n\nSeeds should not automatically form the entire diet because many seed-only diets are nutritionally unbalanced and can be high in fat. Provide fresh water daily and wash fresh produce properly. Avoid avocado, chocolate, caffeine, alcohol, and foods containing excessive salt. Consult an avian veterinarian before making major dietary changes.",
        "Cage Setup & Enrichment": "A parrot's cage should provide opportunities for climbing, playing, resting, and exploring. Different perch textures and diameters help support foot health. Position food and water containers where they remain clean and accessible, away from areas where droppings may contaminate them.\n\nProvide bird-safe toys that encourage chewing, problem-solving, and exploration. Rotate toys regularly to prevent boredom, while keeping familiar items available. Avoid toys containing dangerous small parts, loose threads, or toxic materials. Parrots also need supervised time outside the cage to exercise and interact with their surroundings.",
        "Training & Social Interaction": "Training provides mental stimulation and helps parrots develop positive relationships with their owners. Start with simple activities such as responding to their name, targeting, or stepping onto a hand. Use small rewards and praise to encourage desired behaviours.\n\nTraining sessions should be short, calm, and enjoyable. Avoid punishment, shouting, or forcing a parrot to perform. Social interaction is important because parrots are highly intelligent and many species are naturally social. Provide daily opportunities for communication, play, and enrichment while allowing sufficient rest.",
        "Health & Grooming": "Parrots require regular health monitoring and veterinary examinations with an avian veterinarian. Observe their appetite, droppings, breathing, activity, feather condition, and body weight. Birds often hide illness, so even subtle changes can be important.\n\nFeather care includes access to appropriate bathing or misting opportunities, depending on the bird's preferences. Nail and beak care should be assessed by an experienced professional. Never trim flight feathers or attempt beak trimming without appropriate professional guidance. A healthy diet, clean environment, and regular veterinary care support long-term wellbeing.",
    },
})

NEW_LEARNING_CONTENT.update({
    "lovebirds": {
        "Understanding Lovebirds": "Lovebirds are small, energetic parrots known for their playful personalities and social behaviour. They enjoy climbing, chewing, exploring, and interacting with their environment. Each lovebird has its own personality, and its behaviour may vary depending on its age, experiences, and living conditions.\n\nLovebirds communicate through chirping, body movements, feather positions, and beak interactions. They may become territorial around their cage or favourite spaces. Understanding their natural behaviours helps owners provide appropriate care and avoid unnecessary stress. Lovebirds need daily attention, enrichment, and opportunities for physical activity.",
        "Preparing a Suitable Cage": "Lovebirds need a secure and spacious cage that allows them to move freely, climb, and stretch their wings. Select an enclosure with safe bar spacing, suitable perches, and enough room for toys and feeding containers. Place the cage in a comfortable, well-ventilated location away from kitchen fumes and extreme temperatures.\n\nInclude bird-safe chew toys, swings, and different perching surfaces. Clean food and water containers regularly and remove contaminated materials. Lovebirds also require supervised exercise outside the cage in a secure environment. A suitable cage arrangement supports their activity and reduces boredom.",
        "Feeding & Nutrition": "Lovebirds need a balanced diet containing appropriate protein, fats, vitamins, minerals, and fibre. A suitable formulated pellet diet can provide essential nutrients, while fresh vegetables and limited quantities of appropriate fruits add variety. Seed mixes should not be the only source of nutrition.\n\nProvide fresh drinking water every day and clean feeding containers regularly. Avoid avocado, chocolate, caffeine, alcohol, and salty or sugary foods. Monitor your lovebird's weight and eating habits because changes may indicate nutritional problems or illness. An avian veterinarian can recommend an appropriate diet for your bird.",
        "Bonding with Your Lovebird": "Building a relationship with a lovebird requires patience and consistent interaction. When introducing a new bird, give it time to become familiar with its surroundings. Speak gently, move slowly, and allow it to approach you voluntarily.\n\nOffer suitable treats as positive reinforcement and gradually introduce simple hand interactions. Avoid forcing the bird to sit on your hand or attempting to catch it unnecessarily. Some lovebirds become comfortable with physical affection, while others prefer interacting through play and vocal communication. Respecting individual preferences helps establish trust.",
        "Communication & Behaviour": "Lovebirds communicate using chirping, calling, body movements, and different vocal expressions. They may become louder when excited, seeking attention, or responding to sounds around them. Changes in feather position, posture, and activity can provide additional clues about their emotional state.\n\nA relaxed lovebird may preen, play, or rest comfortably. Aggressive lunging, repeated biting, or defensive postures may indicate fear or territorial behaviour. Observe your bird in different situations to understand its individual communication patterns. Sudden behavioural changes should be evaluated by an avian veterinarian.",
        "Socialisation & Enrichment": "Socialisation helps lovebirds become comfortable with people, sounds, toys, and everyday household activities. Introduce new experiences gradually and reward calm behaviour. Avoid exposing them to loud noises or unfamiliar animals without proper supervision.\n\nProvide opportunities for climbing, chewing, foraging, and problem-solving. Rotate safe toys and hide small portions of suitable food in bird-safe enrichment materials. Lovebirds may be kept alone or with compatible companions, but introducing another bird does not guarantee friendship. Monitor interactions carefully and provide separate resources when necessary.",
        "Health & Hygiene": "Maintaining hygiene is essential for preventing illness in lovebirds. Clean food and water bowls daily, remove droppings and spoiled food, and regularly clean cage surfaces and toys. Provide suitable bathing opportunities to support feather cleanliness.\n\nObserve your lovebird's weight, appetite, droppings, breathing, feathers, and activity. Warning signs include fluffed-up feathers for prolonged periods, sitting at the bottom of the cage, unusual discharge, breathing difficulty, and reduced eating. Contact an avian veterinarian promptly when illness is suspected. Regular checkups help detect problems early.",
    },
    "doves": {
        "Understanding Dove Behaviour": "Doves are generally gentle birds known for their soft cooing sounds and calm behaviour. They communicate through vocalisations, body posture, feather movements, and social interactions. Some doves enjoy living with compatible companions, while others may need individual space depending on their temperament.\n\nDoves spend time walking, resting, foraging, and preening. They may become nervous when approached suddenly because they are naturally alert to potential threats. Provide a peaceful environment and observe their behaviour regularly. Understanding their natural habits helps create a comfortable relationship between the bird and its caregiver.",
        "Preparing a Comfortable Habitat": "Doves need a spacious enclosure that allows them to move, stretch their wings, and exercise. Provide suitable perches, resting spaces, clean flooring, and safe nesting areas when appropriate. The enclosure should protect them from predators, extreme weather, and environmental hazards.\n\nMaintain good ventilation without exposing birds to strong drafts. Place food and water containers in clean, accessible locations. Avoid overcrowding and provide enough space for compatible birds to interact peacefully. A comfortable habitat supports physical health and reduces unnecessary stress.",
        "Feeding & Nutrition": "Doves generally require a balanced diet containing appropriate seeds, grains, protein, vitamins, and minerals. A suitable commercial dove or pigeon feed can provide the nutritional foundation. Additional dietary requirements may vary depending on age, breeding status, and health condition.\n\nFresh drinking water should always be available. Food must be stored properly to prevent mould and contamination. Avoid feeding excessive quantities of human snacks or unsuitable food scraps. Monitor body condition and consult an avian veterinarian to ensure that the diet provides adequate nutrition.",
        "Building Trust with Doves": "Building trust with doves begins with creating a peaceful and predictable environment. Spend time near the enclosure and speak gently so the birds become familiar with your presence. Avoid sudden movements or attempts to catch them unnecessarily.\n\nOffer suitable food and allow the birds to approach voluntarily. Some doves may become comfortable eating near their caregivers, while others prefer maintaining a greater distance. Respect their individual personalities and avoid forcing physical affection. Regular gentle interactions help develop familiarity and confidence.",
        "Social Interaction & Bonding": "Doves may develop strong social relationships with compatible birds and can also become familiar with their caregivers. Observe their interactions to understand whether they are comfortable sharing the same environment. Peaceful cooing, resting nearby, and mutual preening may indicate positive social relationships.\n\nProvide sufficient space, feeding areas, and resting locations to reduce competition. Introduce unfamiliar birds gradually and monitor them carefully. If aggressive behaviour occurs, separate the birds safely. Social compatibility depends on individual temperament, so companionship should never be forced.",
        "Breeding & Nesting Care": "Doves may form breeding pairs and use suitable nesting areas to raise their young. Responsible breeding requires healthy adult birds, suitable housing, proper nutrition, and a quiet environment. Provide appropriate nesting materials and avoid disturbing the pair unnecessarily.\n\nParents generally participate in feeding and protecting their chicks. Young doves require careful monitoring as they grow and develop feathers and movement skills. Breeding should only be undertaken when the caregiver can provide suitable long-term care. Consult an avian veterinarian if chicks appear weak or are not being fed properly.",
        "Health & Hygiene": "Maintaining a clean environment is essential for dove health. Clean feeding and drinking containers regularly, remove spoiled food, and keep the enclosure dry. Check the birds' feathers, eyes, nostrils, feet, and droppings for unusual changes.\n\nWarning signs include reduced appetite, difficulty breathing, unusual discharge, loss of balance, and prolonged inactivity. Quarantine new birds before introducing them to existing companions. Arrange regular veterinary checkups and consult an avian veterinarian whenever signs of illness appear.",
    },
    "pigeons": {
        "Understanding Pigeon Behaviour": "Pigeons are intelligent and social birds that can recognise familiar people and develop strong routines. They communicate through cooing, head movements, body posture, and wing behaviour. They may become comfortable with familiar surroundings and show interest in their caregivers.\n\nPigeons naturally spend time walking, exploring, foraging, and interacting with other pigeons. They also require opportunities for safe movement and exercise. Observe their behaviour to understand when they are relaxed, frightened, or uncomfortable. Learning their natural habits helps owners create a suitable living environment.",
        "Preparing a Safe Loft": "A pigeon loft should provide enough space for movement, resting, and nesting. It must offer good ventilation while protecting birds from rain, extreme temperatures, predators, and other environmental dangers. Use safe flooring and provide suitable perches and nesting areas.\n\nKeep the loft clean and dry to reduce the spread of parasites and infectious diseases. Ensure that food and water containers are protected from droppings. Avoid overcrowding because it can increase stress and disease transmission. A well-maintained loft supports healthy and comfortable living conditions.",
        "Feeding & Nutrition": "Pigeons need a balanced diet containing suitable grains, seeds, protein, vitamins, and minerals. Commercial pigeon feed mixes are designed to meet different nutritional requirements, depending on age and activity. Breeding birds and growing young pigeons may have different dietary needs from adult companion pigeons.\n\nProvide clean drinking water every day and avoid feeding mouldy or contaminated grains. Suitable mineral supplements and grit may be needed depending on the diet and veterinary advice. Avoid relying entirely on random household food scraps. A balanced feeding routine helps support healthy feathers, muscles, and overall development.",
        "Building Trust with Pigeons": "Building trust with pigeons takes patience and regular positive interaction. Begin by spending quiet time near their enclosure so they become familiar with your presence. Move slowly and avoid sudden gestures that may frighten them.\n\nOffer suitable food in a calm and controlled manner. Gradually allow pigeons to approach your hand if they are comfortable. Never chase or grab them unnecessarily. Consistent feeding routines and gentle handling help pigeons recognise their caregivers and develop confidence in their surroundings.",
        "Flight & Exercise": "Pigeons require regular physical activity to maintain strong muscles and overall fitness. Depending on their living conditions and health, they may benefit from access to a secure flight area or supervised exercise. Flight opportunities should only be provided when the environment is safe and the birds are appropriately trained and prepared.\n\nNever release unfamiliar pet pigeons into an unknown outdoor environment. They may become lost, injured, or unable to find food and shelter. Provide safe movement opportunities while considering weather, predators, and local regulations. Regular observation helps identify changes in activity or physical ability.",
        "Breeding & Nesting Care": "Pigeons may form breeding pairs and establish nesting areas. Responsible breeding requires suitable housing, healthy breeding birds, appropriate nutrition, and enough space for parents and young birds. Provide safe nesting materials and a quiet environment that allows the pair to raise their chicks without unnecessary disturbance.\n\nBoth parent pigeons usually participate in caring for their young. Young pigeons depend on their parents for feeding and protection during early development. Monitor the health of adults and chicks without repeatedly disturbing the nest. Seek veterinary guidance if parents abandon chicks or young birds show signs of illness.",
        "Health & Hygiene": "Pigeon health depends on proper nutrition, clean housing, fresh water, and regular observation. Watch for changes in appetite, droppings, breathing, feather condition, and movement. Common health concerns include respiratory infections, parasites, injuries, and digestive problems.\n\nClean the loft regularly and remove accumulated droppings and spoiled feed. Quarantine newly introduced birds and seek veterinary advice before mixing them with existing pigeons. Wash your hands after handling birds or cleaning their environment. Consult an avian veterinarian if a pigeon becomes unusually inactive, stops eating, or develops abnormal breathing.",
    },
})

